data.content:
Here are the three breaking changes in EF Core 10 and fixes:

## 1. `SetProperty` now requires two type arguments

In EF Core 10, `SetProperty` gained an additional method-level type argument for the entity type. Your manual expression tree must pass both:

```csharp
// EF Core 9: typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) }
// EF Core 10: include the entity type as the first type argument
setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>>(
    Expression.Call(
        instance: setters.Body,
        methodName: nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty),
        typeArguments: new[] { typeof(Blog), typeof(string) },  // <-- added Blog
        arguments: new Expression[]
        {
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Constant("foo"), blogParameter)       // <-- value must be lambda now
        }),
    setters.Parameters);
```

The value argument also changed from a raw constant to `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>` — wrap your constant in a lambda.

---

## 2. Complex type column naming convention changed

EF Core 10 no longer auto-prefixes complex property columns with the navigation name. If `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street` both exist, you'll get a runtime conflict. Fix by explicitly configuring column names:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, sa =>
    {
        sa.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street");
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ba =>
    {
        ba.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street");
    });
});

// For nested: Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb =>
    {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ba =>
        {
            ba.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

Alternatively, use the `ComplexTypePropertyDiscoveryConvention` opt-in to restore the old prefix behavior if available.

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` signature changed

EF Core 10 added a `CommandSource` parameter to the interface methods. Update your implementation:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // <-- new parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

Add the `CommandSource commandSource` parameter to every method you implement from this interface. The enum tells you whether the command originated from `SaveChanges`, `LinqQuery`, `ExecuteUpdate`, etc.

---

**Summary:** The expression tree change is the subtlest — both the type arguments array and the value argument shape changed. The other two will surface as compile errors that guide you to the fix.
